---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import FpgaCanvas from '../../components/FpgaCanvas.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout slug="fpga">
  <FpgaCanvas slot="scene" />

  <section>
    <h2>The idea</h2>
    <p class="lead">What if the computer on your desk wasn't really there?</p>
    <p>
      This thesis replaces the CPU, RAM and GPU on your desk with a cheap FPGA board, and moves all the actual
      computing to a server in the cloud. Your keyboard, mouse and monitor stay exactly where they are.
    </p>
    <p>
      The FPGA acts as a thin client. It captures keyboard and mouse events, streams them over Ethernet to the
      server, and receives the display output back in real time. From the user's side, it's just a computer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <div class="defs">
      <div class="def"><strong>FPGA</strong><span>Captures keyboard and mouse input and drives the display. The hardware bridge on the user's desk, written in VHDL.</span></div>
      <div class="def"><strong>Ethernet</strong><span>Two-way channel. Input events travel to the server; rendered frames travel back.</span></div>
      <div class="def"><strong>Cloud server</strong><span>A C# application that replays the incoming input events and streams the resulting display back.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>No expensive general-purpose hardware at the endpoint</li>
      <li>FPGAs are built for I/O: efficient, low-latency, low-power</li>
      <li>Upgrade the cloud hardware without touching the client device</li>
      <li>Applies to IoT, embedded systems and thin-client infrastructure</li>
      <li>Hardware-software co-design: VHDL on the FPGA, C# on the server</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>
      Final-year thesis for a B.Eng. in Electronic &amp; Computer Engineering at the Technical University of Crete,
      spanning embedded systems, real-time networking, hardware description languages and systems programming.
    </p>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
